user_prompt:
Variables are listed below in random order. Use the exact id strings.
[
  {
    "id": "V01",
    "description": "lung cancer",
    "states": "yes|no",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V02",
    "description": "tuberculosis",
    "states": "yes|no",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V03",
    "description": "smoking habit",
    "states": "yes|no",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V04",
    "description": "recent visit to Asia",
    "states": "yes|no",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V05",
    "description": "bronchitis",
    "states": "yes|no",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V06",
    "description": "positive chest X-ray result",
    "states": "yes|no",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V07",
    "description": "dyspnoea (shortness of breath)",
    "states": "yes|no",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "V08",
    "description": "indicator that the patient has tuberculosis or lung cancer (or both)",
    "states": "yes|no",
    "state_count": "2"
  }
]

Task: propose a causal order prior. Earlier variables are potential causes or upstream conditions; later variables are downstream effects, measurements, symptoms, decisions, or aggregate outcomes.

Return one JSON object with this schema:
{
  "ordered_nodes": ["exact id 1", "exact id 2", "..."],
  "confidence": 0.0,
  "high_confidence_pairs": [
    {"before": "exact id", "after": "exact id", "confidence": 0.0}
  ],
  "rationale": "brief, no more than 80 words"
}

Constraints: ordered_nodes must contain every provided id exactly once; do not invent nodes; if evidence is weak, still provide the best semantic prior and lower the confidence.

raw_response:
{
  "ordered_nodes": ["V04", "V03", "V02", "V01", "V05", "V08", "V06", "V07"],
  "confidence": 0.84,
  "high_confidence_pairs": [
    {"before": "V04", "after": "V02", "confidence": 0.97},
    {"before": "V03", "after": "V01", "confidence": 0.92},
    {"before": "V02", "after": "V08", "confidence": 0.9},
    {"before": "V01", "after": "V08", "confidence": 0.9},
    {"before": "V08", "after": "V06", "confidence": 0.86},
    {"before": "V05", "after": "V07", "confidence": 0.8}
  ],
  "rationale": "Recent Asia travel can increase tuberculosis risk; smoking can increase lung cancer and bronchitis risk. Tuberculosis and lung cancer are upstream diseases, while the combined indicator summarizes their presence. Chest X-ray is a diagnostic measurement and dyspnoea is a downstream symptom; bronchitis is placed after major disease risks but before symptoms."
}
